 "controlText": "Among the 22 other whole-hour anchors (pooled net R a trade from -0.086 to 0.063, median 0.04), the UTC rule (+0.072R, its 21-hour day) is above 22 of 22, the New York day (+0.044R) above 14 and the Tokyo day (+0.039R) above 10. Hours 21 and 22 UTC are close to the New York day (its anchor is 21:00 or 22:00 UTC with daylight saving). The control is reported only; it decides nothing.",
 "text": "Neither other open does better. Pooled after costs the UTC rule makes +0.072R a trade (n 4169, month t +3.89), the New York day +0.044R (n 4230, t +2.46) and the Tokyo day +0.039R (n 3560, t +1.71); before costs +0.103 / +0.076 / +0.071R. Both variants trail the UTC rule in both windows (per-trade monthly difference New York −0.047 / −0.013R, Tokyo −0.063 / −0.014R), but the confirmation-window paired t (−0.83 and −0.43) is far from the pre-registered bar, so the result is NO DIFFERENCE SHOWN, not WORSE; in discovery the New York day trails at t −2.18 (not a test window). Neither variant clears the parent bar on its own: New York confirmation +0.039R (t +1.56), Tokyo +0.039R (t +1.19); at 2x costs the Tokyo day is +0.007R and the New York day +0.013R against the rule's +0.041R. The Tokyo day also holds 950 of its 3560 trades over the 17:00 New York rollover, where financing (not charged here) and the break-hour spread (hidden by mid candles) would cost more, so its true figure is lower than shown. What the control adds: the effect is not special to 00:00 UTC; days started anywhere from about 00:00 to 12:00 UTC give similar pooled net R (most +0.04 to +0.06R), while days started 18:00-22:00 UTC (around the New York close) lose. The 00:00 UTC rule with its 21:00 exit is the best of the three tested here mainly because it stops before the rollover hours (the same 00:00 UTC day held 24 hours gives +0.046R). A before-cost tilt common to many anchors is not an edge, and none of these settings is chosen from this reading. Paper research; nothing live changes.",
 "data": "OANDA mid USD/JPY M5, 2005-01-02 → 2026-11-21 (1644641 candles)"
}
